WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.203
A neuron's layer is one record of its birthday.

2
00:00:07.143 --> 00:00:08.703
What else does a birthday leave behind?

3
00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:13.853
Each layer's neurons carry identity genes that serve as markers.

4
00:00:14.893 --> 00:00:17.293
Markers read identity without knowing position.

5
00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:22.497
In mouse, Tbr1 is high in subplate and layer 6 neurons.

6
00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:29.230
Ctip2 and Fezf2 are high in layer 5 neurons that project below the cortex.

7
00:00:30.383 --> 00:00:35.203
Satb2 marks callosal neurons, which send axons to the other hemisphere.

8
00:00:36.163 --> 00:00:37.383
Most sit in the upper layers.

9
00:00:38.417 --> 00:00:42.417
Expression overlaps, and position alone does not define a class.

10
00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:48.527
The cortical schedule so far describes excitatory neurons made by dorsal

11
00:00:48.527 --> 00:00:50.947
progenitors. They release glutamate.

12
00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.170
Cortical interneurons release GABA.

13
00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:59.690
In rodents, most are born in the ganglionic eminences of the ventral forebrain.

14
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.420
They travel up into the cortex.

15
00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:08.500
For most cortical neurons in rodents, a dorsal birthplace means excitatory, a

16
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:09.780
ventral one inhibitory.

17
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:15.650
Interneurons from the medial ganglionic eminence follow a similar birthdate rule,

18
00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:17.190
settling inside out.

19
00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:19.390
Not every interneuron does.

20
00:01:20.433 --> 00:01:22.113
One question is still open.

21
00:01:22.113 --> 00:01:25.693
Does a mammalian progenitor run a clock like the fly's?

22
00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:32.767
Predict. Grow one early progenitor alone in a dish, with no cortex around it.

23
00:01:33.307 --> 00:01:34.887
Does it still make neurons in order?

24
00:01:40.917 --> 00:01:44.117
Sally Temple's laboratory did this in 2006.

25
00:01:45.197 --> 00:01:47.937
The clones made deep-layer markers before later markers.

26
00:01:49.017 --> 00:01:51.077
That points to a schedule within the lineage.

27
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.840
Relatives of the fly factors do have timing jobs in mouse.

28
00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:00.080
But a full cascade like the fly's has not been shown in cortex.

29
00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:02.470
It could be a cascade.

30
00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:07.650
Or it could be a gradual change in competence, shaped by feedback from neurons

31
00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:08.650
already made.

32
00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:13.650
Now return to the one idea.

33
00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:19.030
The progenitor's state at a birthday sets the neuron's temporal identity, its layer

34
00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:20.030
included.

35
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.040
The pulse dated birthdays.

36
00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:27.700
The ferret grafts showed laminar fate is set late, cues included, and that the menu

37
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:28.700
shortens.

38
00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:32.153
The fly showed what a temporal state can be made of.

39
00:02:32.673 --> 00:02:35.273
And Q counted how one pool is built and then spent.

40
00:02:38.283 --> 00:02:40.843
The takeaway: a birthday is a timestamp.

41
00:02:41.583 --> 00:02:45.783
What the progenitor can make then, and the cues it reads, set the neuron's temporal

42
00:02:45.783 --> 00:02:46.303
identity.
